I am planning to deploy a yolo box - a dedicated hardware unit for
running attended agents.

In terms of config, it will be pretty similar to any other machine I
have in here. I'd like to think through how to design this, and start by
running it in a VM (although not actually running the coding
agents). Basically get the config set up so that when the box arrives, I
can deploy it quickly.

One thing about that: it doesn't have any kind of IPMI, so I plan to set
it up this way:

- put it on a desk with no network connection, attach a monitor, and
  dd a boot image into place
- power off and move it into its actual location hooked up to the network

This means that the config that creates the boot image should be
complete with all the network config etc, so that it can boot with no
monitor.

The plan is to give coding agents root privileges on the host. It's
considered a completely untrusted host, the only output of it is patches
that I review.

I would like to see how much I can lock it down from the kernel
standpoint. I know that rsync.net provides VMs with root on them, and
they do some locking down, I just don't know what it is exactly.

I'm imagining two different approaches:

1. Use security levels to prevent system files from changing after it
   runs.

2. The reboot -r mechanism, where it boots to a userland basically
   running nothing, and then reboots into another userland with all the
   services (could be combined with 1).

I have pf on my firewall, but I would like to have pf on the device
block output as well. I don't know if that's feasible assuming that it
has root - but it seems plausible to me that secure level could also
affect pf, so it's possible to create pf rules that are loaded on boot
but can't be changed after the fact.

We need to think about how we can best lock down a device while still
granting root access to an untrusted user.

Also need to think about general architecture of the running system, and
how it interacts with repos. I want it to operate on lab, or
freebsd-src, or freebsd-ports, or any number of repos. I want there to
be isolation across concurrent agents. I'm thinking of checking out the
repos to zfs datasets, and then cloning those datasets into place in
jails that actually run claude. I might even use nullmount so they're
read only.

I think there still needs to be a zfs dataset that gets cloned, which is
for the claude config, because multiple agents sharing it will want to
modify the settings. The idea is that I log in on the host, with claude
config in a dataset, and then clone that dataset everywhere. Although if
I can tell the tools not to write, or use an env var to tell them where
to write, then I could do a read-only null mount for this as well.

Thinking through this flow a bit, I want deterministic tools that will
launch a jail with whatever repos it needs, perform the work, and clean
up after itself. Every prompt should be done in a clean, repetable environment.
